<h2>The case in outline</h2>
<p>The applicant was in judicial custody for about ten months in FIR No. 34/2025 of the Cyber Police Station (West), Hari Nagar. The complainant&rsquo;s account was a familiar one in online fraud matters: contact through a dating application, a growing relationship of trust, and then an invitation to invest through a trading app, followed by the disappearance of the money. The prosecution&rsquo;s link to the applicant was financial — Rs 1,40,000 of the cheated amount was traced to his bank account. His defence was that the transaction was a failed business deal and that the account had been opened on the advice of a co-accused.</p>

<h2>What the status report revealed</h2>
<p>Justice Girish Kathpalia examined the status report and found that the investigation had, in substance, stopped moving while the applicant remained in jail. The Court noted four gaps in particular:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Co-accused not arrested.</strong> No serious effort had been made to arrest the co-accused named in the investigation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>A possibly fictitious player.</strong> Another co-accused had not been traced at all, and the Court observed that the possibility of that person being fictitious could not be ruled out.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Notices without follow-up.</strong> The investigating officer had issued notices to the managers of the dating application but had taken no further steps on their non-response.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Basic evidence not collected.</strong> Even the complainant&rsquo;s own call logs and chat screenshots had not been obtained in ten months.</div>
</div>
<p>Against that record, the State&rsquo;s submission that the investigation was continuing could not carry the weight of continued detention. The Court also dealt with the submission that the applicant was wanted in a separate cyber-fraud case in Jaipur, holding that the pendency of another case elsewhere was not a sufficient reason to deny bail in this FIR.</p>

<h2>The order</h2>
<p>Bail was granted on a personal bond of Rs 50,000 with one surety in the like amount, to the satisfaction of the trial court or the duty magistrate. The Court directed that a copy of the order be sent to the Jail Superintendent, and made clear that its observations were confined to the bail application and would not influence the trial court&rsquo;s independent assessment.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<p>Bail courts routinely face two competing narratives: the accused points to the length of custody, and the State points to the gravity of the allegations and the continuing investigation. This judgment shows the court resolving that contest not on rhetoric but on the paper trail. A status report is not a formality; it is the evidence by which the State justifies detention. Where it discloses months of inactivity on steps as elementary as collecting the complainant&rsquo;s phone records, the argument for custody weakens regardless of how serious the allegation sounds.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For the defence:</strong> a chronology of the investigation&rsquo;s actual steps — arrests made, notices issued, evidence collected, and the dates of each — is often more persuasive than arguments on the merits, which bail courts in any event cannot finally decide.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For complainants:</strong> the same logic underlines the importance of pressing the investigating agency to complete electronic evidence collection early, because delay can both weaken the eventual trial and loosen custody.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">Under the new criminal codes in force since 1 July 2024, cheating is covered by Section 318 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (formerly Section 420 IPC), criminal conspiracy by Section 61 (formerly Section 120B IPC), and common intention by Section 3(5) (formerly Section 34 IPC). Regular bail in sessions-triable and other serious cases is governed by Section 483 of the BNSS, 2023, the successor to Section 439 CrPC. Every bail matter turns on its own record, and nothing here is a prediction of outcome in any other case.</div>
